From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 06:59:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.web: Make Windows.Data.Json behave as on Windows 11.

All of this was measured on Windows 11 (build 29671) with a probe that
asks each question directly, and the conformance test now passes there.

An object keeps its members in the order they were added; a value given
to a name it has takes the old one's place, and a name removed and
added again comes last.  Stringify writes them in that order.  (Windows
iterates over them in the order of its hash table; that order is not
reproduced, iteration keeps the order of addition too.)  A view of up to
16 members does not split, a bigger one gives its first 16.

Any change to an object or an array ends the views and iterators taken
before it: they answer E_CHANGED_STATE, and an iterator moved past its
end answers E_BOUNDS.  Replacing a value counts, removing a name that is
not there does not, and answers S_OK.  The other users of the vector
helper keep their snapshots.

A failed TryParse still hands out a value: an empty object, an empty
array, a null.  A failed Parse leaves its out-parameter alone.  An array
answers a NULL out-parameter with E_INVALIDARG, where the others answer
E_POINTER.

Numbers are written as printf's "%.15G" when that reads back as the
same number, else "%.17G", with the exponent's leading zeros left out:
1E+21, 1E-5, 0.33333333333333331, -0.  What is not a number comes out
as the old C runtime wrote it, 1.#QNAN, -1.#IND, 1.#SNAN, 1.#INF.
Strings escape control characters as \u00XX in capitals.  Both are read
and written in the C locale.  A number that is too big, or too small to
be anything but zero, is WEB_E_INVALID_JSON_NUMBER.

Parse reads values nested 512 deep, Stringify writes arrays and objects
1024 deep.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

